\title{Dependent function types}
\import{prelude}

\p{Coln is a dependently typed language; this means that the definition of a type is permitted to refer to elements of other types. In fact, we have seen this a lot already without remarking on it, in that later fields of a record are permitted to refer to earlier ones when describing their type. From [[001D]], we had:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} PopulatedSantaList := \kw{sig}
  Naughty : Set
  Nice : Set
  veruca : Naughty
  charlie : Nice
\kw{end}
}

\p{This is already a dependent record: the \em{type} of the field \code{veruca} refers to the \em{element} \code{Naughty}, which only exists because we declared it to previously.}

\p{Besides dependent records, Coln also includes dependent functions; these are functions where the type of the output is permitted to depend on the element that the function is provided as input. The syntax for these is \code{(x : A) -> B}, where \code{B} allowed to mention \code{x}.}

\p{There are especially useful when dealing with families of types:}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} SantaLists := \kw{sig}
  list : Set
  naughty : list -> Set
  nice : list -> Set

  ironic-comeuppance : (l : list) -> naughty l -> String
\kw{end}
}

% Instance example

\p{Here, we wish to store an outcome for each of the naughty children, regardless of which list they are on. Again this is stored as a three-column table. The improvement over a non-dependent function is that the constraints ensure that each entry of the \code{naughty} column is correctly paired with the \code{list} element that it belongs to.}

\p{We are not restricted to simply using variables when constructing a dependent type.}

\pre{%
\kw{theory} Foo := \kw{sig}
  state : \kwi{Set}
  problem : state -> \kwi{Set}
  next : state -> state

  warning-message : (s : state) -> problem (next s) -> \kwi{String}
\kw{end}
}

\p{Here this gives, for every state and every problem that is associated with the \em{next} state, a string.}
